  "mother": {
    "preparation": {
      "model_calls": 1,
      "phase": "preparation",
      "plan": {
        "A": "Research a sequential max-heap for the k best points, reducing the current per-point scan of up to k entries to O(P log k), where P is the number of points. Preserve the reference distance calculation and use input encounter order to break equal-distance ties, matching its stable insertion behavior. Compare with the current method for small k; consider a general, measurement-supported hybrid only if it helps beyond the observed cases. Match the reference’s majority-vote tie rule.",
        "B": "Explore a parallel top-k reduction: divide the input’s point sequence into contiguous chunks, have each worker maintain a private top-k, then merge those candidates and vote. Retain each point’s global encounter order so equal distances and vote ties match the reference. Cap parallelism at the target’s four CPU cores; measure whether the extra setup and merge work pays off. Avoid a shared heap in the hot loop.",
        "C": "Explore an in-place introselect/selection approach over candidate records containing distance, label, and global encounter order. Partition to the exact best k, then compute the majority with the reference’s label tie rule. Use a comparator that preserves encounter order for equal distances, and choose a selection method robust to adverse input order. Measure its extra storage and performance against the heap approach; do not assume it wins for every k or input size.",
        "G": "Integrate only candidates with correctness and timing evidence. The supplied baseline is correct on 4 cases and averaged 2.0657 seconds over three runs on a 4-core ARM64 target; there are no measured optimization variants or input-size profiles, so treat all proposed gains as hypotheses. Preserve behavior for all valid inputs, including distance and label ties. Validate each candidate and measure the integrated program itself: gains may not combine, and a hybrid may be slower than its parts. Do not alter tests, oracle, compiler, budgets, target, permissions, or source outside the authorized integration.",
        "analysis": "The measured run establishes a correct baseline, not a winning optimization: it averaged 2.0657 seconds across three samples, with correctness on 4 cases, on ARM64 with 4 CPU cores. In the supplied code, each point may scan and shift up to k candidates, followed by sorting k labels; this makes top-k selection a plausible bottleneck. A, B, and C investigate distinct sequential-heap, parallel-reduction, and selection approaches. Stable distance ties and the reference’s label-vote tie behavior matter for correctness. No performance profile or variant measurements are provided, so G should choose by measured end-to-end results rather than assume any approach or combination is faster."
      },
