    "synthesis": {
      "model_calls": 1,
      "phase": "synthesis",
      "plan": {
        "A": "Refine the measured sequential max-heap approach, the strongest observed candidate at 0.0294 s versus the 2.0657 s baseline. Explore heap and distance-loop improvements while preserving the original selection semantics: encounter order for equal distances, placeholder labels when fewer than k points exist, and the existing vote tie behavior. Check edge cases, including any valid non-finite coordinates, before claiming equivalence.",
        "B": "Research alternative or adaptive top-k selection strategies, especially general workload-based choices for k relative to the total point count. The measured distance-batching variants (2.046 s and 2.083 s) did not improve on baseline, so do not repeat that approach without new evidence. Compare candidates independently and preserve exact tie and placeholder behavior for every valid input.",
        "C": "Investigate whether parallel distance processing can beat the sequential heap on the 4-core ARM64 target. The measured OpenMP candidate was 0.0298 s, close to but slightly slower than the sequential heap; a second parallel variant was 0.0365 s. Focus on reducing scheduling and merge overhead, use parallelism only where measurements show it amortizes, and preserve deterministic encounter-order ties and all original edge-case semantics.",
        "G": "Integrate the best correct programs from A, B, and C, using the sequential heap as the initial reference candidate because it had the best observed time. Measure each candidate and any combinations directly: gains may not compose, and the observed allocation-arena and vectorization experiments were near baseline when measured independently. Retain only changes that pass the full correctness checks and improve measured performance; do not specialize to test values or alter the computation.",
        "analysis": "The baseline scans points while maintaining a sorted top-k array, with O(Pk) candidate updates for P points. The measured heap candidate reduced time from 2.0657 s to 0.0294 s (about 70×); the first parallel heap candidate measured 0.0298 s, while later storage and batching experiments measured about 2.02–2.08 s. These measurements favor the heap but do not establish that other changes combine with it or that small timing differences are robust. Preserve the source’s stable equal-distance selection, label sorting and vote tie rules, and missing-label placeholders, and validate all valid inputs."
      },
